# 2026-07-30 implementation notes
- The typer's existing take-tombstone machinery (moved_vars/moved_fields,
  snapshot/restore, branch union merge, loop re-move check, rebind revive
  via clear_all_moved_for) is now the single ownership analysis. New
  MoveReason variants carry the diagnostic story: AssignMove(owner, span)
  for M1 and Sent(span) for M9.
- M1: record_take_moves_in_stmt (renamed duty, same hook) marks a plain
  `b is a` bind/assign of an aggregate-typed Var as a move. Drop-side
  accounting needed NO change — collect_consumed_ids already excluded
  the source of every plain bind (8-6), so runtime semantics were
  already move; only the use-side rejection was missing.
- Aggregate category (type_is_aggregate, lower/block.rs): Vec, Map,
  Coroutine, Generator, and structs/enums/tuples/arrays transitively
  containing one. NOT String (value, deep-copies), NOT Channel/ActorRef
  (runtime-refcounted handles), NOT @resource (own linear discipline).
- M3: a plain bind of an aggregate struct field canonicalizes its
  access_mod to Take in the Bind lowering, so the MIR FieldClear
  tombstone and moved-field diagnostics apply identically to
  `v is b.items` and `v is take b.items`. The moved-field diagnostic is
  reworded to "use of moved field `b.items`" (no longer blames a `take`
  the user never wrote).
- M4: element binds are detected by HIR kind (VecMethod/MapMethod
  get/peek/front/back/first/last, Index) + resolved aggregate type —
  NOT via is_aliased_read_of_heap, whose unresolved-ty test misses
  inferred-element containers (that's why the first cut didn't fire).
- M10: Stmt::Defer registers its read var-ids (defer_read_vars, never
  cleared — DefIds are unique; over-approximates by design). Every move
  site routes through mark_var_moved_checked, which rejects moves of
  defer-read vars. collect_hir_var_ids_expr gained Builtin/VecNew/
  Coerce/StrictCast/Ternary/ChannelSend arms — log(x) lowers to Builtin
  and was invisible to the collector.
- Ported from the deleted src/ownership/ (the rest was dead or subsumed:
  borrow counting never released, DoubleMutableBorrow unreachable,
  fn_ret_types write-only):
  - return-of-borrowed: `return %local` of an Owned var → error in the
    Ret arm of the record pass, wording kept for the audit_alpha corpus.
  - channel-send moves: ChannelSend of an aggregate Var → Sent tombstone.
- while-loops now run check_loop_body_moves (they silently skipped it;
  for/loop/sim-for had it).
- Driver unification: OwnershipVerifier blocks deleted from both
  pipelines; pipeline.rs (build/run/test) now runs HirValidator (it
  never did); `jinn check` runs the full typer + HirValidator + fold and
  rejects the §3.3 program it used to pass.
- Fallout in-tree was tiny and all real: two typer unit tests needed a
  non-strict resolve (lambda binds hit strict mode via the new early
  resolve); std/bangle.jn and std/dataframe.jn had 16 genuine M4
  element-alias binds, fixed with `copy`.
- Follow-ups, deliberately out of scope:
  - `copy` of Map (and Vec-of-Map etc.) does not clone — is_value_clonable
    excludes Map, so the MIR Copy arm skips the Clone inst and aliases
    silently. Every M1/M4 diagnostic names `copy` as the escape hatch, so
    this gap now matters more; needs its own task.
  - Lambda captures of aggregates are not yet moves (M11 is about
    generators; the old verifier's capture-move was the only enforcement
    and had no test coverage). 8-8 (cross-task capture) is the natural
    place to settle capture semantics.
  - Consuming-param inference still under-approximates lambda bodies /
    block exprs / named-arg calls (consume_infer.rs docs); the
    flow-sensitive analysis catches the use-after-move side, but a
    conditional escape still leaks rather than double-frees (union
    merge is deliberately conservative toward leak-not-corrupt).
